No builtin base attr fallback for V::Obj. Could add after cls.lookup:
```
for k in ob.cls.mro.borrow().iter().skip(1) {
  if k.module=="builtins" && k.name=="object" {
     if let Some(v)=builtin_class_attr(k,name) { return Ok(bind v) }
  }
}
```
But builtin_class_attr generic fallback returns a descriptor for ANY name, so would make hasattr every name true for all objects (bad). Need object-specific method whitelist and only call builtin_class_attr for known names. We can add special-case match in V::Obj getattr for object dunder methods we need: `__lt__`, `__le__`, `__gt__`, `__ge__`, `__eq__`, `__ne__`, `__hash__`, `__repr__`, `__str__`, maybe `__format__`, `__reduce__`, `__sizeof__`, `__init__`, `__new__`, `__class__`, `__delattr__`, `__setattr__`, `__dir__`, `__getattribute__`, `__subclasshook__`? But minimal for conformance ABC tests: rich comparisons and maybe `__hash__` already handled via special hashing? `hasattr(obj, "__hash__")` in validate_isinstance? Not used. `hasattr(instance, "__eq__")` etc needed. `hasattr(instance, "__ne__")` needed for Mapping; `__lt__/le/gt/ge` needed. Set defines its own except __ne__; object __ne__ needed for Set too.
`operator` calls `op(instance, other)` via builtin operator functions using `cmp_v`/binop, not directly getattr? For `operator.__ne__` we'll implement comparison via `cmp_v`, which invokes rich comparison semantics and will find instance methods? If we add object getattr fallback but operator uses cmp_v which uses `rich`/`binop` and may not use getattr. Need inspect `cmp_v`.

